  "hints": [
    "You need to answer 'are a and b already connected?' while links keep being added.",
    "A disjoint-set union (union-find) tracks which component each server is in.",
    "For each link, find both roots. Equal roots means the link is redundant; otherwise union them. Path compression and union by size make operations nearly constant time."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function firstRedundant(n, links) {\n  const parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i)\n  const size = new Array(n).fill(1)\n  const find = x => {\n    while (parent[x] !== x) {\n      parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n      x = parent[x]\n    }\n    return x\n  }\n  for (let i = 0; i < links.length; i++) {\n    let a = find(links[i][0])\n    let b = find(links[i][1])\n    if (a === b) return i\n    if (size[a] < size[b]) [a, b] = [b, a]\n    parent[b] = a\n    size[a] += size[b]\n  }\n  return -1\n}\n",
    "py": "def first_redundant(n, links):\n    parent = list(range(n))\n    size = [1] * n\n\n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n\n    for i, (a, b) in enumerate(links):\n        a, b = find(a), find(b)\n        if a == b:\n            return i\n        if size[a] < size[b]:\n            a, b = b, a\n        parent[b] = a\n        size[a] += size[b]\n    return -1\n",
    "explanation": "Union-find keeps one root per component. A link joining two servers with the same root closes a cycle, so it is redundant. Path halving in find and union by size keep trees shallow."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O((n + m) · α(n))",
    "space": "O(n)",
    "notes": "α is the inverse Ackermann function, at most 4 for any realistic n. Running BFS after every link would cost O(m · (n + m))."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "Links can also be removed. Why is union-find no longer enough, and what do you use offline?"
  ]
}
